WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:10.948
Two pieces of timber have to cross, and
finish flush. This is how that is done.

2
00:00:11.448 --> 00:00:17.810
A halving joint. Each member loses exactly
half its thickness where they meet.

3
00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:30.244
Take half off one, half off the other, and the two faces come together
level. That is the whole idea, and everything else is accuracy.

4
00:00:30.744 --> 00:00:37.153
Three tools do this job. A try
square, to prove a line square.

5
00:00:37.373 --> 00:00:43.178
A marking gauge, to score the same
distance from a face every time.

6
00:00:43.398 --> 00:00:52.686
And a tenon saw: fine teeth for a clean cut across the
grain, and a stiff back that keeps the cut straight.

7
00:00:53.186 --> 00:01:00.105
Start with a prepared piece. Planed
straight, planed square, gauged to size.

8
00:01:00.325 --> 00:01:10.124
One wide face is marked as the face side. One
edge, square to it, is marked as the face edge.

9
00:01:10.344 --> 00:01:19.400
Every measurement from here comes off those two surfaces.
Not off a sawn end, and not off whichever side is nearest.

10
00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:25.055
Take the width from the piece that
has to fit, not from the drawing.

11
00:01:25.275 --> 00:01:35.352
Square the shoulder line across the face, and down both edges.
The stock of the square presses hard against the face edge.

12
00:01:35.572 --> 00:01:43.746
If the stock lifts even slightly, the line is not
square, and neither is anything you cut to it.

13
00:01:44.246 --> 00:01:51.629
Set the gauge to half the thickness. Lock it,
then measure it again before it touches the wood.

14
00:01:51.849 --> 00:01:59.466
Score the cheek line with the fence riding on the
face side, on both members, from the one setting.

15
00:01:59.686 --> 00:02:11.249
One setting used twice keeps the halves equal. A gauge a millimetre out
puts the joint two millimetres out, because the error lands on both.

16
00:02:11.749 --> 00:02:18.762
Now hatch the waste. This is the last mark
before the first cut that cannot be undone.

17
00:02:18.982 --> 00:02:25.344
It takes seconds, and it is why nobody
who does it ever saws off the wrong half.

18
00:02:25.844 --> 00:02:34.760
The saw takes out a kerf about a millimetre wide.
That millimetre has to come out of the waste.

19
00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:44.083
Saw on the waste side and the line stays on the work.
The joint is full size, and a shaving brings it home.

20
00:02:44.303 --> 00:02:52.615
Saw down the middle of the line and half the kerf comes
out of the joint. It is slack, and nothing puts wood back.

21
00:02:53.115 --> 00:03:03.332
Pare the waste with a chisel, cutting from both faces towards
the middle, so the far edge is supported and does not tear out.

22
00:03:03.552 --> 00:03:12.608
Then fit it dry. If it needs a mallet it is too
tight: take a shaving off the cheek and try it again.

23
00:03:12.828 --> 00:03:20.119
It should go together by hand and stay
there. Check both diagonals before any glue.

24
00:03:20.619 --> 00:03:27.538
Face side and face edge. Shoulder
squared, cheek gauged, waste hatched.

25
00:03:27.758 --> 00:03:35.421
Sawn on the waste side, pared to the line,
fitted dry. In that order, every time.
